  "title": "2.2. Solving a Sturm-Liouville problem",
  "body": " 2.2. Solving a Sturm-Liouville problem  MTH 401    A general Sturm-Liouville problem has the form where are the extreme boundary conditions of . Solving a problem means finding the the eigenvalues , , that correspond to non-trivial solutions, and then find the corresponding solutions or eigenfunctions  associated to each .     We'll mostly focus on the simpler form of the problem: (not always called !):      Consider the homogeneous heat equation when the ends are insulated, i.e.     First apply separation of variables; that is, look for a solution with the form This will enable us to write the partial derivatives of in terms of simpler ordinary derivatives of and . Find in terms of , substitute into the heat equation, and rearrange the equation so that all occurrences of are on one side and all the occurrences of are on the other. This leads to a separation constant . You should then find (a) an ordinary differential equation for , (b) an ordinary differential equation for , and (c) two boundary conditions for .      The differential equation and boundary conditions for are called a Sturm-Liouville problem. As in the video, look for eigenvalues and eigenfunctions by considering     Case 1 : so that . The differential equation has solutions that are linear combinations of a growing and decaying exponential.       Case 2 : so the differential equation for is .       Case 3 : so that . The differential equation has solutions that are linear combinations of a sine and a cosine function.        Summarize your findings by stating the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions to this Sturm-Liouville problem:      When , sketch the graphs of the eigenfunctions corresponding to the three smallest eigenvalues.      For each eigenvalue, solve the differential equation for .      Write the general solution to the original heat equation with boundary conditions given on the first page.     "
