  "title": "2.14. Bessel functions of the first kind",
  "body": " 2.14. Bessel functions of the first kind  MTH 401    Recall that Bessel's equation of order is Solutions are found using the Method of Frobenius, that is assuming it has the form for some unknown and solving for the coefficients.  For Bessel's functions of the first kind we take . We find that all odd coefficients are zero ( ), the coefficient is a free variable, and the other even coefficients are for . The solution to the order equation is denoted .      Find a solution to Bessel's equation of order 1 that is bounded at . Use Mathematica to draw a graph of it.      We are not limited to integers. Find a solution to Bessel's equation of order 3\/2 that is bounded at . Use Mathematica to draw a graph of it.     Fun facts!    Bessel functions of integer order show up when solving problems in cylindrical coordinate systems, and but half-integer orders of Bessel functions ( ) appear in spherical problems.    When solving Bessel's equation of order , if we take then we will find Bessel's functions of the second kind , which we denote .     Hankel functions are complex functions that come from combining Bessel functions of the first and second kind, so are Hankel functions of the first kind, and are of the second kind. (We won't get into these...)     "
